// A member that does not exist on a **program-declared** type is caught at check time, whether it is
// spelled as an associated call, an instance call, or a field read. The method set of a `struct` /
// `class` / `enum` written in the program is closed — checking is whole-program, so a call that
// resolves to nothing here resolves to nothing at run time either.
//
// Before this, all three type-checked clean and failed only on Run — and the associated-call form
// failed with `cannot find <Type> in this scope`, which blames the type rather than the member and
// so reads as a missing import.
//
// An in-scope **type parameter** is a `Type::Named` too and stays lenient: a bound licenses methods
// rather than closing the world, and a method no bound declares is documented to defer to runtime
// dispatch. `takes_any` below is the positive control for that — it must not be flagged.
// expect: error E0007 at 34:5
// expect: error E0007 at 35:5
// expect: error E0007 at 36:5
// expect: exit 1
struct P {
    host: string = "h"

    fn local(): P {
        return P {};
    }

    fn label(): string {
        return "p:" ~ self.host;
    }
}

fn takes_any<T>(x: T): T {
    return x;
}

echo P.local().label();
echo takes_any(1);
a = P.nope();
b = P.local().missing();
c = P.local().not_a_field;
